<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neretva</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neretva is a river of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> that flows about 225 kilometres from the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostar">Mostar</a> into the Adriatic in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. In antiquity its valley held the Daorsi and the Roman trading town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narona">Narona</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it gave its name to the Narentines, Slav seafarers whose principality of Pagania was notorious for piracy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neretva.jpg" alt="Neretva" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JERRYE AND ROY KLOTZ MD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NERETVA_RIVER_VALLEY_NEAR_POCITELJ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
